=== .ai/app.http.controllers rules ===

# App/Http/Controllers guidelines

- Controllers are CRUD by design: their only public methods are `index`, `create`, `store`, `show`, `edit`, `update`, and `destroy`.
- Never add a custom method (`approve`, `pin`, `hold`, `export`, `fulfil`). Instead, find the resource being created, updated, or deleted, and give it its own controller:
  - Approving an order → `OrderApprovalController@store`. Rejecting it → `OrderRejectionController@store`.
  - Pinning an order → `OrderPinController@update`. Unpinning it → `OrderPinController@destroy`.
  - Holding an order → `OrderHoldController@store`. Releasing it → `OrderHoldController@destroy`.
  - Approving the selected orders in the fulfilment queue → `FulfilmentController@update`.
- When an endpoint really does not fit CRUD, that is the sign to use an invokable controller with a single `__invoke()` method, named after the thing, minimally: `ExportController`, not `PersonalDataExportController` or `ExportPersonalDataController`.
- Controller names are nouns, never verbs: `OrderStarController`, not `StarOrderController`.
- Controllers stay thin: resolve the form request, call one action (or query), and return a response.
- Controllers never authorize or validate anything: no `abort()`, `abort_if()`, `abort_unless()`, `throw_if()`, `throw_unless()`, `$request->validate()`, `Validator::make()`, or `ValidationException`. Every check lives in the form request of that endpoint.
- `tests/Unit/ArchTest.php` enforces this.

<code-snippet name="Example controller" lang="php">

<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

namespace App\Http\Controllers;

use App\Actions\CreateOrderStar;
use App\Actions\DeleteOrderStar;
use App\Http\Requests\CreateOrderStarRequest;
use App\Http\Requests\DeleteOrderStarRequest;
use App\Models\Order;
use App\Models\User;
use Illuminate\Container\Attributes\CurrentUser;
use Illuminate\Http\RedirectResponse;

final readonly class OrderStarController
{
    public function store(CreateOrderStarRequest $request, #[CurrentUser] User $user, Order $order, CreateOrderStar $action): RedirectResponse
    {
        $action->handle($user, $order);

        return back();
    }

    public function destroy(DeleteOrderStarRequest $request, #[CurrentUser] User $user, Order $order, DeleteOrderStar $action): RedirectResponse
    {
        $action->handle($user, $order);

        return back();
    }
}

</code-snippet>

=== .ai/app.http.requests rules ===

# App/Http/Requests guidelines

- Never use policies or gates: no `app/Policies`, no `Gate::define()`, no `can:` middleware, no `$user->can()`, no `$this->authorize()`.
- Authorization and validation only ever happen in form requests: authorization in `authorize()`, validation in `rules()`. Never in controllers, actions, or models. This includes `index` and `show` endpoints.
- Controllers and actions never check anything themselves: no `abort()`, `abort_if()`, `abort_unless()`, `throw_if()`, `throw_unless()`, `Validator::make()`, `$request->validate()`, or `ValidationException`.
- Create a dedicated form request for each endpoint that checks anything, and never share one between endpoints. If the endpoint has none yet, create one.
- Name it after the controller method it serves, CRUD by design: `index` → `List…Request`, `show` → `Show…Request`, `store` → `Create…Request`, `update` → `Update…Request`, `destroy` → `Delete…Request`. For example `OrderPinController@update` → `UpdateOrderPinRequest`, `OrderPinController@destroy` → `DeleteOrderPinRequest`, `OrderApprovalController@store` → `CreateOrderApprovalRequest`.
- When hiding something should look like it does not exist, return `Response::denyAsNotFound()` from `authorize()` instead of `false`.
- Put input interpretation in form request methods (`$request->tab()`, `$request->parent()`), and complex checks in `App\Rules` validation rules.
- `tests/Unit/ArchTest.php` enforces this.

<code-snippet name="Example form request" lang="php">

<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

namespace App\Http\Requests;

use App\Models\Order;
use App\Models\User;
use Illuminate\Auth\Access\Response;
use Illuminate\Foundation\Http\FormRequest;

final class CreateOrderNoteRequest extends FormRequest
{
    public function authorize(): Response
    {
        $user = $this->user();
        assert($user instanceof User);

        $order = $this->route('order');
        assert($order instanceof Order);

        return $order->isVisibleTo($user) ? Response::allow() : Response::denyAsNotFound();
    }

    /**
     * @return array{}
     */
    public function rules(): array
    {
        return [];
    }
}

</code-snippet>

=== .ai/resources.js.http rules ===

# Frontend HTTP requests

- Every call to an API route goes through `useHttp()` from `@inertiajs/vue3`. Never `fetch()`, never `XMLHttpRequest`, and never an HTTP library: no `axios`, no `ky`, no `got`, no `superagent`. Axios is not a dependency of this app and must not become one.
- Use the documented `get()`, `post()`, `put()`, `patch()`, `delete()`, or `submit()` methods with the endpoint URL and optional lifecycle callbacks. For application routes, use the Wayfinder route object's `.url(...)` when passing the endpoint to these methods. These methods return a promise that resolves with the parsed JSON response; do not pass the route object itself.
- Initialize request data on the hook, for example `const form = useHttp({ name: '' })`, then update `form.name` before calling `form.post(url)`. Request data is sent from the hook instance.
- Read state off the hook instead of tracking it by hand: `processing`, `errors`, `hasErrors`, `wasSuccessful`, `recentlySuccessful`, `progress`, and `isDirty`. Validation errors are exposed through `errors`; `cancel()` aborts, `optimistic()` updates data before the response lands, and `withPrecognition()` enables real-time validation.
- `useHttp` is for JSON endpoints and performs no Inertia visit: the page does not change. Page transitions and form submissions that should navigate keep using `router` and `useForm`.
- `resources/js/composables/useTwoFactorAuth.ts` is the reference implementation in this app.

<code-snippet name="Example useHttp calls" lang="ts">

import { useHttp } from '@inertiajs/vue3';
import { search } from '@/actions/App/Http/Controllers/SearchController';

const http = useHttp({ query: '' });

async function search(): Promise<void> {
    const results = await http.get(search.url(), {
        onSuccess: (data) => {
            console.log(data);
        },
    });

    console.log(results);
}

</code-snippet>

=== .ai/resources.js rules ===

# Frontend UI stability

Every UI change keeps the layout still. Nothing may flash, jump, clip or reflow on first load, navigation, polling, or after an action.

## Loading

- A skeleton occupies exactly the loaded content's box: same height, line count and wrapping at every breakpoint, including the narrow aside. It is visible in light and dark mode, popovers included.
- Delay skeletons by about 300ms so fast responses show nothing. Show one only while data is genuinely missing, never while refetching data you already have.
- Shared props that persist across pages (aside, nav counts) use `Inertia::defer(...)->once()`, so client visits don't refetch them and flash.
- Polling and refresh buttons use partial reloads (`only: [...]`) of the page's own props. Never reload the whole page, and keep showing the old data until the new data arrives.

## Changing content

- Numbers, counts and countdowns use `tabular-nums` in fixed-width slots.
- Buttons whose label changes ("Place order", "Placing…") get a `min-w`. Disabled, loading and enabled states are the same size.
- Conditional UI (banners, "@x is reviewing", inline confirms, errors) reserves its space or overlays. An inline confirm fits in the slot of the control it replaces.
- Removed list rows collapse with the existing order-deletion animation, keeping scroll position and focus.
- Right after an action, the control under the cursor must not flip to a destructive state, e.g. "Place order" → "Cancel order" before the pointer leaves.

## Overflow

- Dialogs and popovers are capped to the viewport (`dvh`) and scroll inside, with a sticky header and footer when actions would scroll away. No double scrollbars and no page scroll through the overlay. Scroll lock must not shift the page: keep `scrollbar-gutter: stable`.
- Long usernames, URLs, reasons, queue names and messages use `min-w-0` with `truncate` or `line-clamp`, and the full text in `title`.
- Auto-growing textareas are `overflow-x-hidden`. Inputs use at least 16px text below `md` so iOS doesn't zoom.
- On mobile, toasts sit above the bottom nav.

## Verify before calling it done

- Check in a browser at 1440×900, 1366×650 and 390×844, in light and dark.
- Measure layout shift (`PerformanceObserver` with `layout-shift`) for first load, navigation, a polling cycle and every action you added. The target is 0.
- Compare side by side with `production` using the same demo data. Record before/after clips for any fix to motion or loading.

